CUtils - Unix-style file commands for AmigaDOS
==============================================

WHAT IT IS

  Five small commands - ls, cp, mv, mkdir and rm - for fingers that
  type `ls -la` faster than they can think `List`. Written in Amiga
  E, one standalone binary each; copy them into C: and go. The name
  works twice: they are the core utils your muscle memory expects,
  and they are the utils that live in C:.

  Requires Kickstart 2.04 or newer.

THE COMMANDS

  ls [-1ahlrRSt] [path | pattern ...]
    Multi-column listing sized by asking the console itself, colors
    on interactive output, .info files hidden like dotfiles (-a
    reveals), hsparwed long format with filenotes, human sizes,
    date/size sorts, safe -R recursion (soft-links never descended,
    hard-link cycles skipped).

  cp [-fr] FROM ... TO
    Patterns, multiple sources, recursive trees. Skips existing
    targets by default (-f replaces, SameLock-guarded); protection
    bits, datestamp and filenote carried like Copy CLONE. Copying a
    directory into its own subtree is refused.

  mv [-fb] FROM ... TO
    Rename() on the same volume - files AND directories, one cheap
    call. Across volumes: copy+delete with full metadata, whole
    directory trees too, all-or-nothing (a failed copy wipes the
    partial destination and leaves the source untouched). -b backs
    an existing target up as <name>.mvbak first.

  mkdir [-p] DIR ...
    -p builds every missing parent in place; an existing directory
    is not an error, a file in the way is reported at the component
    actually blocking.

  rm [-rfv] FILE | PATTERN ...
    Directories need -r; trees delete contents-first, deepest
    directory first. -f strips the d-bit protection and retries -
    force, the Amiga way. Volume roots are refused unconditionally;
    links are deleted as links, never followed.

SHARED MANNERS

  Bundled Unix flags, hand-parsed on purpose; `cmd ?` prints usage.
  Every command matches its own AmigaDOS patterns. Non-destructive
  by default - skips are listed at the end, and nothing ever
  silently clobbers. One bad item never kills a batch; return code
  is the worst that happened: 0 clean, 5 skipped, 10 failed, 20
  break. Ctrl-C honoured throughout. Trees are always walked with
  explicit work lists, never native recursion.

  Each command keeps its own version (see the $VER strings);
  per-command reference docs ride along as <command>.doc, readable
  with More.

ABOUT

  Part of the AmigaTools collection, written in Amiga E:

    https://github.com/creep-ltx/AmigaTools
